(Bug #43 — a `Return` that sits inside an `If` lives in the conditional's
 body, not the function's, so its declared type never reached the
 signature and every caller read the result as a plain integer. For a
 `value` that was a segfault: codegen skipped the callee's tag load, so
 the caller stored whatever r11 happened to hold — the parameter's text
 tag — over an integer payload, and printing it dereferenced 99 as a
 `char*`. For the plain-type family it was a silent wrong answer: a text
 pointer printed as a number. Both directions of the value case, the text
 family, the single-expression form that always worked, and a branch that
 does not fire are pinned here.)

To score with a value called v.
  If v is a number, return a value, v.
  Otherwise, return a value, 99.

To verdict with a number called n.
  If n is greater than 0, Return a text, "big".
  Otherwise, Return a text, "small".

To fallback with a value called v.
  Return a value, 99.

To perhaps with a number called n.
  If n is greater than 0, Return a text, "found it".
  print "no branch fired".

(A text scores the default; a number scores itself. Before the fix the
 first of these two lines segfaulted.)
a value called defaulted is score of "hello".
print defaulted.

a value called scored is score of 7.
print scored.

(The same shape with a declared `text` return: before the fix these
 printed the text's address as a number.)
print verdict of 5.
print verdict of 0.

(The control: one expression, no branch. This always worked, and must
 keep working.)
a value called plain is fallback of "hello".
print plain.

(No branch fires on the second call, so the function falls off its end
 and owes the caller an empty text rather than a stray address.)
print perhaps of 1.
print perhaps of 0.
